{
 "thread": "AWufpgybXXE",
 "subject": "Remove loci, out of Hardy Weinberg equilibrium, from a genclone object",
 "messages_listed": 3,
 "last_activity": "2016-01-03T05:58:35Z",
 "messages": [
  {
   "id": "u0LtTLcCCAAJ",
   "from": "Jennifer Gruhn",
   "author": "2ddfc3a96fd08663",
   "date": "2016-01-01T18:12:18Z",
   "subject": "Remove loci, out of Hardy Weinberg equilibrium, from a genclone object",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hello!  <br><br>I created a gencone object by importing a genalex-structured file.  I have 2937 SNPs across 42 individuals, comprising 6 populations.  (I treat the SNPs as microsatellite loci in the file, so that 1-4 below represents either A, C, G, or T.):<div><br></div><div><div style=\"border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word;background-color:rgb(250,250,250)\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">2937</span><span style=\"color:#000\">  </span><span style=\"color:#066\">42</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> <br> <br> </span><span style=\"color:#606\">Ind</span><span style=\"color:#000\">   </span><span style=\"color:#606\">Pop</span><span style=\"color:#000\">     </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#000\">       </span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span style=\"color:#000\">       </span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> <br> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">4</span><span style=\"color:#000\">     </span><span style=\"color:#606\">Crater</span><span style=\"color:#000\">  </span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#000\">   </span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#000\">   </span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span style=\"color:#000\">   </span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span style=\"color:#000\">   </span><span style=\"color:#066\">4</span><span style=\"color:#000\">   </span><span style=\"color:#066\">4</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">8</span><span style=\"color:#000\">     </span><span style=\"color:#606\">Crater</span><span style=\"color:#000\">  </span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#000\">   </span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#000\">   </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#000\">   </span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span style=\"color:#000\">   </span><span style=\"color:#066\">4</span><span style=\"color:#000\">   </span><span style=\"color:#066\">4</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br></span></div></code></div><br>I just ran the hw.test on the loci making up each population - the genclone object is <b>&quot;myData&quot;</b>:<br></div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word;background-color:rgb(250,250,250)\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">myData</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">hwe</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">pop </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> seppop</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">myData</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">%&gt;%</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> lapply</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">hw</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">test</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> B </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span></div></code></div></div><div><br></div><div>I have several loci out of Hardy Weinberg equilibrium, with p values &lt; 0.05, and I would like to remove them.  The result of the hw.test is a matrix of these p values over the loci.  I am not sure how to remove these same loci, but from the genclone object, since this object has a structure different from a matrix or data frame.  Is it easiest to instead manipulate my original genalex file as a data frame, and then again read this file as a genclone object?  </div><div><br></div><div>Any help would be appreciated.  Thank you!</div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "B-OmrnomCAAJ",
   "from": "Zhian Kamvar",
   "author": "d192ec4b1d416345",
   "date": "2016-01-02T05:07:40Z",
   "subject": "Re: Remove loci, out of Hardy Weinberg equilibrium, from a genclone object",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hi,<div><br></div><div>You can subset loci in a genclone or genind object by specifying the &quot;loc&quot; argument using the names or position of the loci. This is documented in the basics tutorial for adegenet (<a href=\"https://github.com/thibautjombart/adegenet/raw/master/tutorials/tutorial-basics.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://github.com/thibautjombart/adegenet/raw/master/tutorials/tutorial-basics.pdf&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792480844904000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0GH3us9KIz3fx9A6p5XivX\">https://github.com/thibautjombart/adegenet/raw/master/tutorials/tutorial-basics.pdf</a>) and the documentation for accessors (<a href=\"http://rpackages.ianhowson.com/cran/adegenet/man/accessors.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://rpackages.ianhowson.com/cran/adegenet/man/accessors.html&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792480844904000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2fang8gvSiOxJ7OfR8k9eR\">http://rpackages.ianhowson.com/cran/adegenet/man/accessors.html</a>).</div><div><br></div><div>For example, if your data had the first 100 loci in HWE, you could subset it by doing:</div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word;background-color:rgb(250,250,250)\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">myData</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">loc </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#066\">100</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span></div></code></div><br></div><div>You could even subset with a TRUE/FALSE vector specifying which loci you want to keep.</div><div><br></div><div>Hope that helps,</div><div>Zhian</div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br></div><div>On Friday, January 1, 2016 at 10:12:18 AM UTC-8, Jennifer Gruhn wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Hello!  <br><br>I created a gencone object by importing a genalex-structured file.  I have 2937 SNPs across 42 individuals, comprising 6 populations.  (I treat the SNPs as microsatellite loci in the file, so that 1-4 below represents either A, C, G, or T.):<div><br></div><div><div style=\"border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word;background-color:rgb(250,250,250)\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">2937</span><span style=\"color:#000\">  </span><span style=\"color:#066\">42</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> <br> <br> </span><span style=\"color:#606\">Ind</span><span style=\"color:#000\">   </span><span style=\"color:#606\">Pop</span><span style=\"color:#000\">     </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#000\">       </span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span style=\"color:#000\">       </span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> <br> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">4</span><span style=\"color:#000\">     </span><span style=\"color:#606\">Crater</span><span style=\"color:#000\">  </span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#000\">   </span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#000\">   </span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span style=\"color:#000\">   </span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span style=\"color:#000\">   </span><span style=\"color:#066\">4</span><span style=\"color:#000\">   </span><span style=\"color:#066\">4</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">8</span><span style=\"color:#000\">     </span><span style=\"color:#606\">Crater</span><span style=\"color:#000\">  </span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#000\">   </span><span style=\"color:#066\">3</span><span style=\"color:#000\">   </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#000\">   </span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span style=\"color:#000\">   </span><span style=\"color:#066\">4</span><span style=\"color:#000\">   </span><span style=\"color:#066\">4</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br></span></div></code></div><br>I just ran the hw.test on the loci making up each population - the genclone object is <b>&quot;myData&quot;</b>:<br></div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word;background-color:rgb(250,250,250)\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">myData</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">hwe</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">pop </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> seppop</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">myData</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">%&gt;%</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> lapply</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">hw</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">test</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> B </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span></div></code></div></div><div><br></div><div>I have several loci out of Hardy Weinberg equilibrium, with p values &lt; 0.05, and I would like to remove them.  The result of the hw.test is a matrix of these p values over the loci.  I am not sure how to remove these same loci, but from the genclone object, since this object has a structure different from a matrix or data frame.  Is it easiest to instead manipulate my original genalex file as a data frame, and then again read this file as a genclone object?  </div><div><br></div><div>Any help would be appreciated.  Thank you!</div></div></blockquote></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
